gm-method/rendering (ideal cutout-foliage alpha/mip checklist + the two safety gates that make alphaToCoverage safe to add late): the ideal is transparent:false + alphaTest ~0.5, mipmapped trilinear textures, texture.anisotropy raised (grep the whole client for 'anisotropy' -- a codebase can ship with literally zero anisotropy setting, smearing every grazing-angle view), and alphaToCoverage on renderers that really have MSAA (check the LIVE gl context attributes' antialias flag, not a config constant). Two non-obvious safety gates found by adversarial verification before enabling A2C: (1) if the foliage material is SHARED with an impostor/billboard bake that renders into a NON-multisampled render target, A2C during the bake is undefined-ish -- enable A2C only AFTER all bakes complete; (2) keep the hard alphaTest alongside A2C rather than dropping it, because the shadow depth pass has no MSAA and three honors material.alphaTest in its auto depth material -- dropping to ~0.1 would densify leaf shadows and change any future bake's alpha. A2C is GL state keyed independently in three's program cache, so it composes with an existing onBeforeCompile wind-shader chain (customProgramCacheKey) without invalidating it. Mip-alpha erosion (distant mips averaging below the alphaTest threshold, thinning tree canopies) is largely masked by A2C's dithered coverage on desktop; that is usually a better first move than alpha-preserving mip rescaling.